🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗦𝘂𝗰𝗰𝗲𝘀𝘀𝗳𝘂𝗹, 𝗯𝘂𝘁 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗢𝗹𝗱 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 𝗮𝗿𝗲 𝗦𝘁𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗪𝗵𝘆?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “You updated your Kubernetes Deployment with a new image, but the old Pods are still running. How will you troubleshoot it?”

In production, I wouldn’t immediately delete the old Pods. First, I’d check whether the Deployment update was actually applied.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Check the Deployment

"kubectl get deployment -n <namespace>"

Verify the desired and available replicas.

2️⃣ Inspect the Deployment configuration

"kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace>"

Check the container image, rollout status, events, and update strategy.

3️⃣ Check ReplicaSets

"kubectl get rs -n <namespace>"

A new ReplicaSet is normally created when a Deployment's Pod template changes.

4️⃣ Check rollout status

"kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name> -n <namespace>"

If the rollout is stuck, inspect the events and new Pods to identify the cause.

5️⃣ Check Pod images

"kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -o wide"

Confirm which Pods are running the old and new versions.

6️⃣ Investigate the cause

Look for:
🔹 Image or configuration not updated
🔹 New Pods failing to start
🔹 Insufficient cluster resources
🔹 Readiness probe failures
🔹 Rolling update settings such as "maxSurge" and "maxUnavailable"

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

A successful manifest update does not necessarily mean the rollout is complete.

Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods

Understand this relationship before taking recovery action, and always verify that the new version is healthy.
